# on device agents

Offense: can run code, write files, spawn processes. 
Biggest risks: malicious code generation, 
package/dep tampering, privileged escalation, 
and data exfiltration.

Defenses: least privilege, 
network isolation, signed packages,
local EDR/kernel tracing, process whitelisting,
immutable runtime images.

---

Primary Attack Vectors:

- prompt injection: bypassing safety instructions, getting
                    the agent to execute arbitrary code, 
                    reveal details of environment
                    and tooling, and misrepresent actions / cover traces.

- supply chain: the ability to install packages makes the 
                agent able to install malware disguised as an npm/pip package.

- data poisoning: when training model learns to associate certain
                  sequences like code words that jailbreak them. Kind of like the
                  concept behind a "sleeper agent" in the cold war.

- privilege escalation: agent can reveal enough about its 
                        environment to allow for known exploits of
                        the OS version or configuration.

---

Defensive Configurations:

Unprivileged Account (non-admin user): don't run agent as root / admin.

Network Policy: deny by default. Only allow agent to communicate with outbound to 
                audited endpoints.

Dependency Hardening: require signed packages, run installs inside an isolated
                      build continer rather than directly on host.

Filesystem and Exec Controls: mount agent workspace as a restricted directory.
                              Use read only mounts where possible.

Secrets Handling: don't store long term secrets in plain files.
                  use OS keychain, hardware tokens, or agent mediated passthrough.

Interactive Confirmation: require humans to sign off on tasks.

Static analysis for generated code: run any agent generated scripts through linters,
                                    sandboxed static analyzers, malware scanners before
                                    execution. 

Kernel Tracing: continuous monitoring (eBPF) to detect anomalous exec patterns,
                unexpected network sockets. 
